Update Application Image and Settings
Objectives
•	Update applications with minimal downtime.
•	Control deployments using strategies.
•	Separate code, configuration, and data for easier updates.
________________________________________
Key Concepts
1. Externalizing Code, Config, and Data
•	Application code resides in container images.
•	Configuration (ConfigMaps, Secrets) and persistent data are externalized.
•	Enables deployment across environments without changing source code.
•	CI/CD pipelines automate building images and pushing them to registries.
________________________________________
2. Deployment Strategies
OpenShift uses Deployment objects to manage application rollout.
RollingUpdate (Default)
•	Replaces pods gradually (one by one).
•	Both old and new versions coexist during the update.
•	Requires version compatibility between old and new pods.
•	Parameters:
o	maxSurge: How many extra pods can be created above desired replicas.
o	maxUnavailable: How many pods can be unavailable during update.
•	Readiness probes ensure traffic goes only to ready pods.
•	Advantages: Minimal downtime, continuous deployment.
Example:
strategy:
  type: RollingUpdate
  rollingUpdate:
    maxSurge: 25%
    maxUnavailable: 50%
Recreate
•	Stops all old pods before starting new ones.
•	Causes downtime during rollout.
•	Use when:
o	Different versions cannot run together.
o	Performing data migrations.
•	Not recommended for high-availability apps.
Example:
strategy:
  type: Recreate
________________________________________
3. Rollouts
•	Updating Deployment (image, env vars, probes) triggers a rollout.
•	Pausing rollout allows multiple updates in one deployment:
oc rollout pause deployment/myapp
oc set image deployment/myapp nginx-120=...:1-86
oc set env deployment/myapp NGINX_LOG_TO_VOLUME=1
oc set probe deployment/myapp --readiness --get-url http://:8080
oc rollout resume deployment/myapp
•	Create deployment with 0 replicas to configure before starting:
oc create deployment myapp2 --image ... --replicas 0
oc set probe deployment/myapp2 --readiness --get-url http://:8080
oc scale deployment/myapp2 --replicas 10
________________________________________
4. ReplicaSets
•	Each Deployment creates one or more ReplicaSets to manage pods.
•	Updating a Deployment creates a new ReplicaSet.
•	Old ReplicaSets are retained (for rollback) according to .spec.revisionHistoryLimit.
•	Do not manually change ReplicaSets; OpenShift manages them.
Example Output:
NAME                DESIRED   CURRENT   READY
myapp2-574968dd59   0         0         0
myapp2-76679885b9   10        10        10
myapp2-786cbf9bc8   0         0         0
________________________________________
5. Rollback
•	Use ReplicaSets to revert if a deployment fails.
oc rollout undo deployment/myapp2              # Rollback to previous version
oc rollout history deployment/myapp2           # List revisions
oc rollout undo deployment/myapp2 --to-revision 1   # Rollback to a specific revision
•	Avoid floating tags (latest) for rollbacks because the image may change in the registry.
•	Use ImageStreams for stable image references.
________________________________________
6. Monitoring Rollouts
•	oc rollout status deployment/myapp2 → Track deployment progress.
•	Readiness probes ensure only ready pods receive traffic.
•	During a rolling update, multiple ReplicaSets may exist, scaling up new pods while scaling down old pods.
________________________________________
7. Recommended Practices
•	Use RollingUpdate for minimal downtime.
•	Configure readiness probes.
•	Avoid floating tags in production for critical deployments.
•	Pause rollouts for batch updates to avoid multiple sequential deployments.
•	Use ImageStreams to track and reference container images reliably.

